The difference between *hurt* and *grievous hurt* is defined under **Section 116 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023**.

**Grievous hurt** refers to specific kinds of physical injury that are considered serious or severe. The following types of hurt are designated as *grievous hurt*:

- Emasculation;  
- Permanent privation of the sight of either eye;  
- Permanent privation of the hearing of either ear;  
- Privation of any member or joint;  
- Destruction or permanent impairing of the powers of any member or joint;  
- Permanent disfiguration of the head or face;  
- Fracture or dislocation of a bone or tooth;  
- Any hurt which endangers life or which causes the sufferer to be during the space of fifteen days in severe bodily pain, or unable to follow his ordinary pursuits.

All the above are considered *grievous hurt* under this section.

In contrast, **hurt** is a broader term that includes any physical injury, but not necessarily of the severity specified above. For instance, a minor injury or temporary pain may constitute *hurt* but not *grievous hurt*.

Importantly, **Section 117 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023** specifies that a person is said to *voluntarily cause grievous hurt* only if they both intend or know themselves to be likely to cause grievous hurt and actually cause such hurt. This means that mere infliction of hurt without meeting the criteria of *grievous hurt* as defined in Section 116 does not constitute the offence of voluntarily causing grievous hurt.

Thus, the key difference is that *grievous hurt* is a specific, legally defined category of injury under Section 116, while *hurt* is a general term that may or may not meet the threshold of grievous hurt. Only injuries falling under the listed categories in Section 116 are considered *grievous hurt*.

A First Information Report (FIR) is not explicitly defined in the provided legal provisions. However, under **Section 230 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023**, the Magistrate is required to furnish to the accused and the victim (if represented by an advocate) a copy of the **First Information Report recorded under section 173**.

This indicates that the First Information Report is a document recorded by the police under section 173 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023, and it is part of the formal record in a criminal proceeding initiated on a police report.

While the exact contents of the First Information Report are not detailed in the provided provisions, **Section 230** specifies that the FIR must include:

- The **police report**;
- The **First Information Report recorded under section 173**;
- The **statements recorded under sub-section (3) of section 180** of all persons whom the prosecution proposes to examine as witnesses (excluding parts excluded by the police under sub-section (7) of section 193);
- **Confessions and statements** recorded under section 183;
- Any **other document or relevant extract** forwarded to the Magistrate with the police report under sub-section (6) of section 193.

Thus, the First Information Report, as required under **Section 230 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023**, is a formal record that includes the initial details of a cognizable offence, such as the time, place, nature, and circumstances of the incident, and the names of persons involved, as recorded by the police and submitted to the Magistrate.

