The outcome of a cheque bounce case under Section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 depends on several factual and procedural factors, including:

- Whether the cheque was duly presented within six months of its date (or within its validity period, whichever is earlier);  
- Whether the payee or holder in due course issued a written demand notice to the drawer within thirty days of receiving the bank’s return memo;  
- Whether the drawer failed to make payment within fifteen days of receiving the notice;  
- Whether the cheque was dishonoured due to insufficient funds or exceeding the agreed limit;  
- Whether the drawer had a valid reason for the dishonour (e.g., genuine insufficiency of funds, fraud, or misrepresentation);  
- Whether the payee can prove the existence of a legally enforceable debt or liability;  
- Whether the drawer can show that the cheque was not presented in a timely manner or that they suffered actual damage (as per Section 84);  
- Whether the bank’s slip or memo, bearing the official mark, is properly produced to establish dishonour (as per Section 146).

The law does not provide any guarantee of victory. Courts assess each case on its merits, and the burden of proof lies on the complainant (the payee). If the drawer can show that the cheque was not presented in time, or that the funds were genuinely insufficient due to a valid reason, or that the payee failed to follow the required procedural steps (like sending a written notice), the case may fail.

Therefore, **you will not definitely win your cheque bounce case**. The result depends on the facts, evidence, and interpretation by the court.

For a stronger chance of success, ensure:
- The cheque was presented within six months;
- A written demand notice was sent within 30 days of receiving the bank’s return memo;
- The drawer failed to pay within 15 days of receiving the notice;
- All documents (cheque, bank return memo, demand notice, proof of service) are properly filed with the complaint.

If you are considering filing a complaint, it is strongly recommended to consult a licensed advocate who can assess your case and guide you through the legal process.
